Let $\Omega\subset\mathbb R^3$ be any bounded connected domain with $C^\infty$ boundary. For $j=1,2$, let $\lambda_j,\mu_j\in C^\infty(\overline\Omega;\mathbb R)$ satisfy $\mu_j>0$ and $3\lambda_j+2\mu_j>0$ on $\overline\Omega$. If $$\Lambda_{\lambda_1,\mu_1}
 =\Lambda_{\lambda_2,\mu_2}
 \colon H^{1/2}(\partial\Omega;\mathbb R^3)
       \longrightarrow H^{-1/2}(\partial\Omega;\mathbb R^3),$$ then $\lambda_1=\lambda_2$ and $\mu_1=\mu_2$ throughout $\Omega$.

For every smooth pair $(u,d)$, $$\label{eq:augmented-identity}
 Lu=R(u,\mathop{\mathrm{div}}u),\qquad
 \mathop{\mathrm{div}}R(u,d)-S(u,d)=P(\mathop{\mathrm{div}}u-d).$$ In particular, $Lu=0$ implies $R(u,\mathop{\mathrm{div}}u)=S(u,\mathop{\mathrm{div}}u)=0$. The normalized augmented operator $$\label{eq:normalized-augmented}
 \mathcal LU=
 \left(m^{-1}R(u,d),\,
 \ell^{-1}\bigl(S(u,d)-2m^{-1}\nabla m\cdot R(u,d)\bigr)\right)$$ has the form $$\label{eq:laplace-principal-form}
 \mathcal L=\Delta\mathrm{Id}_4+\sum_{j=1}^3 A_j(x)\partial_j+A_0(x)$$ with smooth matrix coefficients. Consequently, the difference of the normalized augmented operators for the two coefficient pairs has order at most one and coefficients supported in $\overline\Omega$.

Let $B$ be a bounded ball. For $U\in C_c^\infty(B;\mathbb C^4)$ and all sufficiently small $h>0$, $$\label{eq:augmented-carleman}
 \left\lVert E_\tau^{-1}U\right\rVert_{H_h^1}
 \le Ch\left\lVert E_\tau^{-1}\mathcal LU\right\rVert_{H_h^{-1}}.$$

For the same $B$ and $\theta$, and all sufficiently large $\tau$, every $w\in C_c^\infty(B;\mathbb C^3)$ satisfies $$\label{eq:physical-test-estimate}
 \left\lVert E_\tau^{-1}w\right\rVert_{L^2(B)}
 \le C\left\lVert E_\tau^{-1}Lw\right\rVert_{L^2(B)}.$$ Moreover, for every $f\in L^2(B;\mathbb C^3)$ there exists $z\in L^2(B;\mathbb C^3)$ satisfying, distributionally, $$\label{eq:physical-solvability}
 E_\tau^{-1}L(E_\tau z)=f\quad\text{in }B,
 \qquad \left\lVert z\right\rVert_{L^2(B)}\le C\left\lVert f\right\rVert_{L^2(B)}.$$

Let $B'\Subset B$. There exists $h_{B'}>0$ such that, whenever $0<h=\tau^{-1}<h_{B'}$ and $z,f\in L^2(B;\mathbb C^3)$ satisfy $E_\tau^{-1}L(E_\tau z)=f$ distributionally in $B$, $$\label{eq:scaled-interior}
 \sum_{j=0}^2 h^j\left\lVert \nabla^jz\right\rVert_{L^2(B')}
 \le C_{B'}\bigl(\left\lVert z\right\rVert_{L^2(B)}
                         +h^2\left\lVert f\right\rVert_{L^2(B)}\bigr).$$ If $f$ is smooth, then $z$ is smooth in $B$ for each fixed $\tau$.

Let $I\subset\mathbb R$ be an open interval and let $A\in C^\infty(D_R^2\times I;\mathbb C^{n\times n})$. For each fixed $t\in I$, the equation $$\label{eq:planar-frame-equation}
 \partial_{\overline z}V=A(z,t)V$$ has a smooth invertible matrix solution on $D_R^2$. For every $R'<R$ and $t_0\in I$, there are an interval $I_0\Subset I$ containing $t_0$ and a smooth invertible solution $V$ on $D_{R'}^2\times I_0$.*


*Given this $V$, a radius $R''<R'$, and $F\in C^\infty(D_{R'}^2\times I_0;\mathbb C^n)$, the equation $$\label{eq:planar-inhomogeneous}
 \partial_{\overline z}v=Av+F$$ has a smooth solution on $D_{R''}^2\times I_0$. If $F(\,\cdot\,,t)=0$ for $t\notin K$, where $K\Subset I_0$ is compact, the solution can be chosen with the same transverse support property. These assertions also hold with $D$ in place of $\partial_{\overline z}$, and for systems obtained by multiplying $D$ by an invertible smooth matrix.

